WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Error handling controls how an application behaves when something goes wrong.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:16.280
That includes invalid input, unavailable dependencies, authorization failures, database errors, timeouts, misconfiguration, and unexpected runtime conditions.

3
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:20.380
Secure error handling does not mean hiding every problem.

4
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:27.600
It means responding predictably, preserving the security posture of the application, and giving the right audience the right amount of information.

5
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:34.520
Logging creates records that help operators, developers, security teams, and auditors understand what happened.

6
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.620
Good logs make routine support easier and incident response faster.

7
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.400
They show important events, outcomes, actors, resources, and timing.

8
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.620
Poor logs do the opposite.

9
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:55.960
They can hide failures, make investigations slow, expose sensitive information, or create noisy records that nobody trusts.

10
00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:01.740
A system can fail securely and still be difficult to operate if its logs are vague or inconsistent.

11
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.540
The challenge is balance.

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.960
Users need clear messages that help them recover without exposing internals.

13
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:12.860
Developers and operators need diagnostics that explain what failed.

14
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.000
Security teams need evidence that supports detection and investigation.

15
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:23.780
Privacy and compliance obligations limit what should be collected and how long it should be kept.

16
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:32.200
Secure error handling and secure logging sit at the intersection of usability, diagnostics, security, privacy, and accountability.
